WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
- All right, let's finish things up

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.210
with one last uplifting story, y'all.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.326
This is What I'm Liking.

00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:07.190
(jazz music)
(audience cheers)

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.750
So it's one of the biggest
cliches of the pandemic, right?

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.280
People make in sourdough.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.860
I mean, I still can't make
it, but other people did it.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.110
Like a lot of people.

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.090
65-year-old Arnie in the Bronx

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:18.390
hopped aboard that bread train

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.950
and it led to a wonderful
and unexpected journey,

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.360
as well as a new business.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.760
So let's get Arnie on the line

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.660
who watches on NBC New York at two.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.201
What's up, Arnie?
(audience cheers)

00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:29.538
- Hey, Kelly.

00:00:29.538 --> 00:00:30.650
Great to see you.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.483
- [Kelly] Oh, it's good to see you too.

00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:32.410
- Thank you for having me.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.700
- Well, I have to start with that.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.450
God, that looks so good
right in front of you.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.730
So let's start with the bread.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:37.610
Let's start there.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.770
How did you, how did this happen?

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.630
- I'm a commercial photographer.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.350
I shoot for businesses and
magazines and even weddings.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.890
And when the pandemic came, I had no work.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:52.440
And I needed something to
do to keep from going crazy.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.850
And I've always loved
sourdough bread, eating it.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.080
And, but I was always
intimidated on how to make it.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.860
And because the first
thing you need is a starter

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.020
and this can take a couple
of weeks to actually develop.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:06.780
And I, was just intimidated.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.710
But then my daughter made
some and gave it to me

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.560
and I started making it.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:12.960
And I gotta tell you,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.180
it was terrible.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.636
It, it was embarrassing
what I was getting.

00:01:16.636 --> 00:01:17.469
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:20.720
But, I have a friend
who's a professional baker

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.270
and I was posting my disasters

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.620
and he kept on giving me tips

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.510
on how to make it better and better.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.180
And some, about a month,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:31.080
I started making breads
that I was really proud of.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.320
And then I found, I love the
process of making the bread

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.700
almost as much as eating it.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.240
And I really like eating sourdough bread.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.450
So I'm making all these loaves

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.050
and I gotta figure out what
am I gonna do with them?

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:44.930
So at first,

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.940
I started bringing 'em to the
local pantry, food pantry,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.840
the Friendly Fridge in the Bronx.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.130
And I was getting feedback that,

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.650
whenever my bread was
there people were so happy.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.610
They loved having it.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.200
So I was getting all
this appreciation for it.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.730
So I was like, "What else
can I do with my bread?"

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.620
And I belonged to a kayak club.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.400
And we have a barbecue night
and I was bringing my bread.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.550
And there were people
there who make honey,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.380
and pickles and all kinds of things.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.370
So I started swapping with them.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.310
And then my favorite item

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.920
is this piece of artwork by Nina Velasquez

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.960
that she made of me

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.550
which has become like my,

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.500
I, you know, the,

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.730
you know, she ID'd me as the
bread baker of Riverdale.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.750
I so appreciate it.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.727
And then, other people were saying,

00:02:31.727 --> 00:02:34.230
"I don't make anything,
but I want your bread.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.320
Can I buy it?"

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.620
And suddenly I found myself
in the bread business.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.057
- Yeah amen, I'm one of those people.

00:02:39.057 --> 00:02:39.890
(audience claps)

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.110
I don't make bread, but I love to eat it.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.950
I love this story, this is so awesome.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.180
My like how life, you know,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.250
it gets hard, but then
it takes a left turn

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.168
and it's like a whole nother
beautiful chapter, so.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:51.070
- [Arnie] Yes.
- Yeah, congratulations, man.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.040
I wanna try your bread.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.350
Thank you, Arnie.
(audience applauds)

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.001
I'm liking your post right now.

00:02:55.001 --> 00:02:55.834
- [Arnie] Thank you.

00:02:55.834 --> 00:02:56.910
- And that's not all, my man,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.360
our season-long partner,
Pilot pen makers of Frixion,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.860
the go-to erasable pen of
"The Kelly Clarkson Show,"

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.900
want to celebrate those
creating fearlessly,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.733
just like you, sir.

00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:06.527
So they're gonna give you
$1,000 just for being you.

00:03:06.527 --> 00:03:08.445
(audience cheers)

00:03:08.445 --> 00:03:09.720
- Oh, woody.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:10.640
Oh my, thank you.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.420
- You can make, you can make
a lot of bread with that.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:13.590
That's all I'm saying.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.240
Feel free to send some here.
- [Arnie] Yes.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.290
- [Kelly] Okay, thank you so much, Arnie.

